const interviewAnswers = [
  {
    prompt: t('Come progetteresti un assistente RAG per procedure controllate?', 'How would you design a RAG assistant for controlled procedures?'),
    short: t(
      'Partirei da identity, purpose e ACL prima del retrieval. Un resolver seleziona la revisione effettiva al timestamp; hybrid retrieval e reranking restituiscono passaggi con lineage. Il modello produce claim brevi con citation ID, mentre validator controllano versione e supporto. In caso di accesso negato, fonti conflittuali o evidence insufficiente, il sistema rifiuta. Valuterei leakage, version accuracy, citation entailment, refusal, latency e tempo utente in shadow mode, con process owner responsabile.',
      'I would begin with identity, purpose, and ACLs before retrieval. A resolver selects the effective revision at the request timestamp; hybrid retrieval and reranking return passages with lineage. The model produces short claims with citation IDs while validators check version and support. The system refuses on denied access, conflicting sources, or insufficient evidence. I would evaluate leakage, version accuracy, citation entailment, refusal, latency, and user time in shadow mode, with the process owner accountable.'
    ),
    long: t(
      `Progetterei l’assistente come un sistema documentale controllato, non come un chatbot con un vector store. Definirei prima la decisione: aiutare un utente autorizzato a trovare la procedura effettiva e comprendere passaggi citati, senza sostituire l’autorità del documento o del process owner. La request include identità verificata, ruolo, sito, purpose e timestamp. Un policy enforcement point autorizza prima di qualunque retrieval e impedisce leakage anche di titoli. Il document resolver usa stato, effective-from, superseded-by e scope per selezionare una sola revisione valida. In caso di record mancanti o conflittuali, restituisce un refusal code e apre un percorso verso Document Control. La pipeline indicizza chunk strutturali con document ID, revision, section anchor, file hash, ACL e asset scope. Combina lexical e dense retrieval perché codici esatti e parafrasi hanno esigenze diverse, poi reranka e applica una threshold calibrata. Il prompt consente soltanto claim sostenuti dai passaggi forniti e richiede citation ID per ogni istruzione. Un verifier controlla appartenenza e versione; reviewer qualificati adjudicano entailment nel pilot. Il golden set comprende answerable, unauthorized, superseded distractor, conflict, missing evidence, due lingue e prompt injection documentale. Hard gate: zero access leakage, zero unsupported operational claim e nessuna versione errata sul gate set. Inizio shadow, poi advisory ristretto con citazioni apribili e stop condition esplicite. Misuro tempo per trovare la fonte corretta, citation correctness, refusal precision e recall, p95, costo per risposta accettata e reviewer time. Versiono corpus, parser, embedding, reranker, prompt e model. Se metadata o governance documentale non sono pronti, finanzio prima quella readiness. Il modello resta un componente probabilistico; authorization, versione e autorità rimangono deterministiche e auditabili.`,
      `I would design the assistant as a controlled document system rather than a chatbot with a vector store. I would first define the decision: help an authorized user find the effective procedure and understand cited steps without replacing document or process-owner authority. Each request carries verified identity, role, site, purpose, and timestamp. A policy enforcement point authorizes before any retrieval and prevents even title leakage. A document resolver uses status, effective-from, superseded-by, and scope to select one valid revision. Missing or conflicting records produce a refusal code and a route to Document Control. The pipeline indexes structural chunks with document ID, revision, section anchor, file hash, ACL, and asset scope. It combines lexical and dense retrieval because exact codes and paraphrases have different needs, then reranks and applies a calibrated threshold. The prompt permits only claims supported by supplied passages and requires a citation ID for every instruction. A verifier checks passage membership and version, while qualified reviewers adjudicate entailment during the pilot. The golden set includes answerable, unauthorized, superseded distractor, conflict, missing evidence, both languages, and document prompt injection. Hard gates are zero access leakage, zero unsupported operational claims, and no wrong version on the gate set. I would begin in shadow mode, then run a narrow advisory pilot with openable citations and explicit stop conditions. Measures include time to the correct source, citation correctness, refusal precision and recall, p95 latency, cost per accepted answer, and reviewer time. Corpus, parser, embedding, reranker, prompt, and model are versioned. If document metadata or governance is not ready, I fund that readiness first. The model remains probabilistic while authorization, version, and authority stay deterministic and auditable.`
    ),
    followUps: [
      t('Come testeresti il refusal behavior?', 'How would you test refusal behavior?'),
      t('Che cosa faresti se il retrieval perde un warning?', 'What would you do if retrieval misses a warning?')
    ]
  },
  {
    prompt: t('Quando useresti un agent loop invece di un workflow deterministico?', 'When would you use an agent loop instead of a deterministic workflow?'),
    short: t(
      'Scelgo un workflow quando passi, gate e side effect sono enumerabili, come validare e creare un work order. Considero un agent loop solo quando il percorso di indagine varia, gli strumenti sono soprattutto read-only e l’adattamento dimostra valore contro una baseline. Definisco state, allowlist, budget, max step, no-progress, handoff ed escalation. Se ruoli e beneficio non sono misurabili, rifiuto la complessità agentica e mantengo il pattern più semplice.',
      'I choose a workflow when steps, gates, and side effects are enumerable, as in validating and creating a work order. I consider an agent loop only when the investigation path varies, tools are mostly read-only, and adaptation demonstrates value against a baseline. I define state, allowlists, budgets, maximum steps, no-progress detection, handoffs, and escalation. If roles and benefit cannot be measured, I reject agentic complexity and retain the simpler pattern.'
    ),
    long: t(
      `La distinzione parte dal control flow. Un workflow deterministico codifica una sequenza e transizioni note. Può chiamare un LLM per estrarre o classificare, ma validation, authorization, approval, scrittura e conferma restano passi espliciti. È la scelta corretta per un work order perché i side effect richiedono idempotenza, audit e gestione di timeout prevedibile. Un agent loop lascia invece al modello scegliere ripetutamente l’azione successiva in base ai risultati. Lo considero per un’investigazione read-only tra maintenance history e segnali sensore quando l’ordine delle verifiche non è noto e la decomposizione può lavorare in parallelo. Anche allora definisco objective, facts con provenance, assumptions, actions attempted, tool allowlist, token e cost budget, massimo step, due-error limit e no-progress detection. I tool con side effect restano fuori dal loop o dietro approval deterministico. Ogni handoff contiene task ID, scope, evidence IDs, open question, prohibited action e acceptance criteria; non trasferisce una conversazione indistinta. Le stop condition includono rubric coperta, conflitto da escalare, budget esaurito o due cicli senza nuovo evidence. Valuto agenti contro one model e deterministic orchestration sullo stesso case set: success adjudicato, evidence completeness, unsupported claims, tool error, elapsed time, total tokens, handoff failure e reviewer time. Faccio fault injection su timeout, stale handoff e risultati contraddittori. Se il vantaggio non è stabile o il coordinamento assorbe il tempo risparmiato, mantengo la baseline. La complessità non è una capability in sé. Il pattern migliore è il minimo che soddisfa outcome, risk e operating model, con fallback semplice disponibile.`,
      `The distinction starts with control flow. A deterministic workflow encodes known sequence and transitions. It may call an LLM for extraction or classification, but validation, authorization, approval, writing, and confirmation remain explicit steps. It is right for a work order because side effects need idempotency, audit, and predictable timeout handling. An agent loop instead lets a model repeatedly choose the next action from results. I consider it for read-only investigation across maintenance history and sensor evidence when the order of checks is unknown and decomposition can usefully run in parallel. Even then I define objective, facts with provenance, assumptions, attempted actions, tool allowlist, token and cost budgets, maximum steps, a two-error limit, and no-progress detection. Side-effect tools remain outside the loop or behind deterministic approval. Every handoff contains task ID, scope, evidence IDs, open questions, prohibited actions, and acceptance criteria rather than an undifferentiated conversation. Stop conditions include rubric coverage, conflict needing escalation, exhausted budget, or two cycles without new evidence. I evaluate agents against one model and deterministic orchestration on the same case set using adjudicated success, evidence completeness, unsupported claims, tool errors, elapsed time, total tokens, handoff failure, and reviewer time. Fault injection covers timeout, stale handoff, and contradictory results. If benefit is unstable or coordination consumes saved time, I keep the baseline. Complexity is not a capability by itself. The best pattern is the minimum satisfying outcome, risk, and operating-model needs, with a simple fallback available.`
    ),
    followUps: [
      t('Quali tool escluderesti da un loop?', 'Which tools would you exclude from a loop?'),
      t('Come dimostreresti il beneficio del parallelismo?', 'How would you demonstrate the benefit of parallelism?')
    ]
  }
]

